#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096546 :question/id #uuid "283e9a4a-443f-579f-b272-248446d3a49f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6233" :question/problem "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=2 \\\\ x-4z &=6 \\\\ -3x+6y &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n\n$$\nM= \\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 & -6\\end{array} \\right].\n$$\n\n\n\nTransforming $M$ into reduced row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the row echelon form above, we have only two pivots (in the $1$st and $2$nd columns). To transform the matrix to reduced row echelon form, we need to create zeros above the pivots only. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-4z &=6 \\\\ y-2z &=2 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation, we can remove this equation from the system. \n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=4z+6 \\\\ y &=2z+2 \\\\ z &=z,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4z+6 \\\\ 2z+2 \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b010a518-7aa3-5b53-9a6d-ef0779ff0dfa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178 :topic/difficulty 0.7261066881298396 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428229 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 3 eligible passed source activities = 0.333333; nearest selected fraction = 1/3. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/3 (1/3). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1374->1047. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267} :encompassing/weight 0.3333333333333333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428230 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 3 eligible 